#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# cylindrical_decomposition

#% text
# `cylindrical_decomposition(formula, [x, y, ...])` writes the solution set of a real polynomial formula as cylindrical cells: conditions on `x`, then on `y` given `x`, and so on.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Bounds are roots of polynomials in the earlier variables: rationals, square roots, or `root` objects.

#% item
# Neighbouring cells whose description above is the same are combined.

#% item
# Quantified variables are eliminated first; the free variables are the listed ones.

#% item
# Every cell's sample point is certified, and the answer is checked at rational sample points before it is returned.

#% item
# `cylindrical_decomposition(formula, vars, mode)` with one or two variables gives the `"Closure"`, `"Interior"`, `"Exterior"`, `"Boundary"` or the list of connected `"Components"` of the set. The cells' adjacency is found exactly from the limits of the bounding curves at the ends of each interval.

#% item
# With the mode `"Function"`, a set that is everything or empty is `true` or `false`; otherwise the call stays. Inside a formula, such a call stands for its set.

#% item
# Coefficients may be real constants such as `sqrt(2)`, `pi` or `log(3)`: they are decomposed as parameters near their values, and the conditions on them are decided by exact comparison. When a constant lies on a boundary between cases, which no enclosure can prove, the call stays.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# The unit disk:

#% cell
cylindrical_decomposition(x^2 + y^2 < 1, [x, y])
#% output
#= -1 < x and x < 1 and -(1 - x^2)^(1/2) < y and y < (1 - x^2)^(1/2)

#% text
# Part of the disk:

#% cell
cylindrical_decomposition(x^2 + y^2 < 1 and y > x, [x, y])
#% output
#= -1 < x and x <= (-1/2)*2^(1/2) and -(1 - x^2)^(1/2) < y and y < (1 - x^2)^(1/2) or (-1/2)*2^(1/2) < x and x < (1/2)*2^(1/2) and x < y and y < (1 - x^2)^(1/2)

#% text
# The other order of the variables:

#% cell
cylindrical_decomposition(x^2 + y^2 < 1 and y > x, [y, x])
#% output
#= (-1/2)*2^(1/2) < y and y <= (1/2)*2^(1/2) and -(1 - y^2)^(1/2) < x and x < y or (1/2)*2^(1/2) < y and y < 1 and -(1 - y^2)^(1/2) < x and x < (1 - y^2)^(1/2)

#% text
# A ball:

#% cell
cylindrical_decomposition(x^2 + y^2 + z^2 < 1, [x, y, z])
#% output
#= -1 < x and x < 1 and -(1 - x^2)^(1/2) < y and y < (1 - x^2)^(1/2) and -(1 - x^2 - y^2)^(1/2) < z and z < (1 - x^2 - y^2)^(1/2)

#% text
# Constant coefficients:

#% cell
cylindrical_decomposition(x^2 + y^2 < pi and x > 1, [x, y])
#% output
#= 1 < x and x < pi^(1/2) and -(pi - x^2)^(1/2) < y and y < (pi - x^2)^(1/2)

#% text
# A curve:

#% cell
cylindrical_decomposition(y^2 == x*(-1 + x^4), [x, y])
#% output
#= x == -1 and y == 0 or -1 < x and x < 0 and (y == -(-x + x^5)^(1/2) or y == (-x + x^5)^(1/2)) or x == 0 and y == 0 or x == 1 and y == 0 or x > 1 and (y == -(-x + x^5)^(1/2) or y == (-x + x^5)^(1/2))

#% text
# A projection:

#% cell
cylindrical_decomposition(exists(y, x^2 + y^2 < 1 and y > x), x)
#% output
#= -1 < x and x < (1/2)*2^(1/2)

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# The boundary of the open disk:

#% cell
cylindrical_decomposition(x^2 + y^2 < 1, [x, y], "Boundary")
#% output
#= x == -1 and y == 0 or -1 < x and x < 1 and (y == -(1 - x^2)^(1/2) or y == (1 - x^2)^(1/2)) or x == 1 and y == 0

#% text
# The closure adds the two points where the parts meet the line y = 0:

#% cell
cylindrical_decomposition(x^4 > x^2 + y^2, [x, y], "Closure")
#% output
#= x < -1 and -(-x^2 + x^4)^(1/2) <= y and y <= (-x^2 + x^4)^(1/2) or x == -1 and y == 0 or x == 1 and y == 0 or x > 1 and -(-x^2 + x^4)^(1/2) <= y and y <= (-x^2 + x^4)^(1/2)

#% text
# The isolated point at the origin is a component of its own and not in the interior:

#% cell
cylindrical_decomposition(x^4 >= x^2 + y^2, [x, y], "Components")
#% output
#= [x < -1 and -(-x^2 + x^4)^(1/2) <= y and y <= (-x^2 + x^4)^(1/2) or x == -1 and y == 0, x == 0 and y == 0, x == 1 and y == 0 or x > 1 and -(-x^2 + x^4)^(1/2) <= y and y <= (-x^2 + x^4)^(1/2)]

#% cell
cylindrical_decomposition(x^4 >= x^2 + y^2, [x, y], "Interior")
#% output
#= x < -1 and -(-x^2 + x^4)^(1/2) < y and y < (-x^2 + x^4)^(1/2) or x > 1 and -(-x^2 + x^4)^(1/2) < y and y < (-x^2 + x^4)^(1/2)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `resolve`, `exists`, `for_all`, `reduce`, `find_instance`
